  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Suppose that $a\\ne0$, $b>0$, $b\\ne1$ and $x>0$. Prove that $$\\log_{b^a}x^a=\\log_b x.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\log_{b^a}x^a=\\log_b x$.\n\nLet $y=\\log_b x$ and write $x^a=(b^a)^y$.",
          "worked": "Let $y=\\log_b x$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=b^y\\\\x^a&=(b^y)^a\\\\x^a&=(b^a)^y\\\\\\log_{b^a}x^a&=y\\\\\\log_{b^a}x^a&=\\log_b x.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, evaluate $\\log_{16}2+\\log_8 2+\\log_4 2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{13}{12}$",
          "worked": "From part (a), $\\log_{2^k}2=\\dfrac1k\\log_2 2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_{16}2+\\log_8 2+\\log_4 2&=\\frac14\\log_2 2+\\frac13\\log_2 2+\\frac12\\log_2 2\\\\\\log_{16}2+\\log_8 2+\\log_4 2&=\\frac14+\\frac13+\\frac12\\\\\\log_{16}2+\\log_8 2+\\log_4 2&=\\frac{3+4+6}{12}\\\\\\log_{16}2+\\log_8 2+\\log_4 2&=\\frac{13}{12}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 37
      }
    ]
  },
